WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.540
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.110
We got some really encouraging comments from
our Suicide Squad autopsy, so we’re trying

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:10.110
it again.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:14.700
This time we’re diving into the trenches
with my latest time-suck: Battlefield 1.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.600
Just a note here, we’re not out to tell
you whether or not Battlefield is fun (it

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.090
is), or if it’s historically accurate (it’s
not), or if snipers are ruining the game (they

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:24.090
might be).

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:28.310
We’re here, instead, in good Wisecrackian
fashion, to delve into the brains of the game.

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:32.260
Battlefield 1 does something that most of
its predecessors never dared to do, and that

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.969
was to say something.

00:00:33.969 --> 00:00:37.800
Now, saying something in a game that’s 90%
getting taunted by 12 year olds

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.300
"...and piss off!"

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.360
is difficult, but Battlefield tries nonetheless.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.390
But does it succeed in saying something?

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:45.390
Well, let’s find out!

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.530
Welcome to this Wisecrack autopsy of Battlefield 1.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.670
Spoiler alert for old War movies that you
probably should have have seen already anyway.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.540
But first, some context.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.820
Setting Battlefield in the First World War
is more meaningful than you might think.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:02.639
Of course, you could say the creators chose
The Great War because they’d run out of

00:01:02.639 --> 00:01:08.500
modern wars after exhausting World War 2,
Vietnam, the present day, the future -- and

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.689
you wouldn’t be wrong.

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:13.939
However, I’d like to argue that Battlefield
1’s setting provides really fascinating

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:17.820
insights into contemporary politics and how
we think about war.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:22.060
If you grew up around the same time I did,
your childhood probably meant weeks of hunkering

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:25.619
down in front of a TV to avoid the sun and
murder Nazis.

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:27.899
So - many - Nazis.

00:01:27.899 --> 00:01:32.409
This was an era marked by games like Call
of Duty, Medal of Honor, and, of course, Battlefield

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:33.649
1942.

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:36.229
World War 2 is a media darling for a reason.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:40.359
No matter where you land on the political
spectrum, we can all agree who the good guys

00:01:40.359 --> 00:01:42.180
were, and who the bad guys were.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:46.990
And while World War 2 has been, and will likely
continue to be, a hugely popular inspiration

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:50.109
for media, it was really popular in the early
2000s.

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:52.180
I don’t think this is entirely coincidental.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.980
Call of Duty released in 2003, the same year
the United States invaded Iraq.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:02.100
Medal of Honor, which launched in ‘99, released
8 of their 11 titles in the four years following

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.670
the start of the Iraq War.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:09.170
Battlefield 1942 released in September 2002,
the same month George Bush formally called

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:14.060
for the ousting of Saddam Hussein and a year
after the US declared a “war on terror”

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.590
in response to 9/11.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:19.910
Meanwhile, this era saw hugely popular movies
like Saving Private Ryan, which went on to

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:24.840
inspire Medal of Honor: Allied Assault, Enemy
at the Gates, Pearl Harbor, and the HBO miniseries

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.150
Band of Brothers.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.590
World War 2 caters to everyone’s needs.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:33.120
For the proponents of the War on Terror and
the Iraq War, which was, at its onset, the

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:38.790
vast majority of people, World War 2 affirms
the story of America-as-savior-of-the-world.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.880
We restored freedom to Europe, and we’re
back to do it again.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.420
"America, f*ck yeah!"

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.820
And for those against
America’s continued involvement in the Middle

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:52.790
East, World War 2 provided a comforting escape
to the good ol’ days when good and evil

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:55.080
were as clear as black and white.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.460
"There are no good wars, with the following exceptions:

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.660
The American Revolution, World War 2, and the Star Wars Trilogy."

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.660
So what does any of this have to do with Battlefield
1?

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:09.420
World War I has never, in recent memory, been
a popular war for media.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.739
But all the sudden it’s featured by a huge
franchise.

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:15.390
What makes this war resonate with gamers now?

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:19.450
Sure its in the midst of its centennial, but it was
a war where millions didn’t even die in

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:24.300
the glory of battle, but from disease; a war
where there are really no good guys or bad guys.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.280
Given our current shift in opinion about American
interventionism,

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:33.170
we no longer see our wars as conflict between
good and evil akin to World War 2, but as

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:36.599
a futile tragedy akin to World War 1.

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:40.450
This sense of tragedy is bolstered throughout
the game by its use of pathos.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:46.019
Pathos is a Greek word for “suffering, feeling,
emotion, calamity,” and, for Aristotle,

00:03:46.019 --> 00:03:48.220
is one of the three modes of persuasion.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:52.650
For a game that’s mostly about its multiplayer,
Battlefield finds a lot of ways to inject

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:53.650
pathos.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.760
And not just the usual “sacrifice for the
greater good” kind, either.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.240
"All we got out here's blind orders - and chance."

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:04.400
For one, instead of infinitely respawning
in the prologue, we’re told that we are

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.890
“Not expected to survive.”

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:10.400
We’re launched into a gray, bleak hellscape,
where every time you die, you’re shown the

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.850
name, birth and death dates of the soldier
who just died.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:17.620
Rather than presenting an endless series of
faceless deaths, the game gives the player

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:22.400
a feeling of pity for the soldiers, and emphasizes
the fact that each death is an individual

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.000
human tragedy.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.500
These mini-obituaries share something with
the original war story — The Iliad — in

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:34.150
which Homer provides a mini obituary for almost
every character that dies, no matter how minor,

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:37.669
in order to make sure the reader doesn’t
forget the cost of battle.

00:04:37.669 --> 00:04:42.080
As thinker Jasper Griffin argues, while many
stories use the “countless insignificant

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.870
dead” as nothing more than a “mighty number
for the hero to slay,” the Iliad draws emotion

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:50.120
from the deaths of even the most insignificant
heroes.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:54.820
In a genre that thrives on hordes of faceless
people to kill - Battlefield 1 sets itself

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:59.180
apart by forcing us to confront the actual
human toll of the war.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:02.990
Even the loading scenes of Operations mode
don’t provide a coherent narrative so much

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:07.900
as explore the emotional lives of soldiers
from both sides: The hollow optimism of Brits

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:19.740
fighting the Ottomans 
and American doughboys fighting the Germans,

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:26.380
And the inevitable confrontation with the
horrors of war.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.300
Battlefield set itself up in a great place:
showcasing the hopes, thoughts and dreams

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:40.640
of soldiers during a war fraught with moral
ambiguity.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:46.760
In the prologue, we see a Harlem Hellfighter
facing off with a German soldier.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.350
They both lower their weapons in resignation.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.430
This sort of thing happened more than you
might realize.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:55.240
You’ve probably heard of the Christmas Truce,
where soldiers from opposing armies stopped

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:57.860
murdering each other and even exchanged gifts.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.199
So, awesome.

00:05:59.199 --> 00:06:03.080
Battlefield sets itself up to explore this
dynamic in its campaign; that Unlike World

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:07.650
War II, we the audience are a little more
primed to feel for the opposing side.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:11.099
But Battlefield quickly abandons this in the
bulk of the single player.

00:06:11.099 --> 00:06:15.889
As soon as you move on to another war story,
you’re faced with actual faceless NPCs for

00:06:15.889 --> 00:06:20.360
you to mow down, and fair enough - feeling
sad for the enemy doesn’t make for very

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:22.860
exciting gameplay in an first person shooter.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:27.070
But if we had our Wisecrack way, it would
be really interesting if the theme embodied

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:30.400
in the Hellfighter stand-off was further developed
in the game.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:33.039
We see similar confrontations explored in
film.

00:06:33.039 --> 00:06:37.280
In World War 1 classics like “Paths of Glory”
and “All Quiet on the Western Front,”

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:40.970
the bad guy is not the enemy, but the war
itself is.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:44.560
In “All Quiet on The Western Front,” a
soldier is met with horrible remorse when

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:47.540
he stabs an enemy combatant in a foxhole.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:53.129
In “Paths of Glory,” a captured German
woman is berated by a bunch of rowdy soldiers

00:06:53.129 --> 00:06:56.420
before they’re overtaken by the beauty of
her voice.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:10.880
The arbitrary division of humanity melts away
as soldiers join her in song.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.940
This is one of my favorite scenes in all of cinema.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:17.820
In World War 2, however, the bad guy is...the
bad guy.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:22.360
Saving Private Ryan, for instance, shows us
an act of mercy gone wrong: After Upham finds

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:26.310
camaraderie with his German enemy, he gets
his friends killed.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:32.690
This fact forces Upham to reevaluate his view
of war, and of Germans.

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:37.180
Despite featuring some stories from the Central
Powers in some of the loading scenes, only

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.530
Allies are playable in the campaign.

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:43.949
This is a tragedy, because the interactivity
of video games can provide an unprecedented

00:07:43.949 --> 00:07:46.500
amount of depth to these kinds of narratives.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.819
What do the stories being told in Battlefield
look like from the eyes of a German or an Ottoman

00:07:50.819 --> 00:07:51.819
soldier?

00:07:51.819 --> 00:07:56.250
Rather than diving into any of the interesting
themes the game sets up, such as: the world’s first

00:07:56.250 --> 00:08:01.139
“industrial” war, its morally ambiguous
nature, and the human cost of battle, we’re

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:05.520
instead given generic stories about self sacrifice
and personal growth.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:09.720
Video games are an incredible art form, one
with tons of untapped potential.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:16.060
And yeah, I get it, the game is about yelling
at people to play the fucking objective.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:19.810
But the creators of Battlefield 1 tried to
do something insightful, and for that, we

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:20.810
commend them.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:24.570
If you make games and are watching this, please
do more of this.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:28.830
But if you’re a lowly gamer like us looking
for a great game that explores the psychological

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:34.440
impact and moral ambiguity of war: play Spec
Ops: The Line. Thanks for watching guys! Peace!

